<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Generality Lands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Generality Lands were territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> governed directly by the States General rather than forming provinces of their own. Conquered mainly from the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> during the Eighty Years' War, they included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-brabant">States Brabant</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeelandic-flanders">States Flanders</a> and parts of Limburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-guelders">Upper Guelders</a>. Their largely Catholic inhabitants had no vote in the States General, and the lands correspond broadly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Limburg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/generality-lands.jpg" alt="Generality Lands" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joostik · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Republiek_der_Zeven_Verenigde_Nederlanden.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>After 1648 there were four main Generality Lands, together about a fifth of the Republic's territory. States' Brabant was the northern part of the Duchy of Brabant, the bulk of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a>. States' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> was the northern part of the County of Flanders, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeelandic-flanders">Zeelandic Flanders</a>, although the largely Protestant lands of Axel and several forts were represented in the States of Zeeland.</p><p>States' Overmaas, beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> as seen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, comprised small territories between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a>, Liège and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, among them the County of Dalhem, Valkenburg Land and the Herzogenrath Land, while Maastricht itself was a condominium with the Prince-Bishopric of Liège. States' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-guelders">Upper Guelders</a>, a part of Spanish Guelders including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venlo">Venlo</a> and Echt, came to the Republic by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> in 1713, when another part went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and a small part around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roermond">Roermond</a> stayed with the Austrian duchy of Guelders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The lands were taken in the later years of the Eighty Years' War, and their possession was confirmed by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> in 1648. Most had no provincial government because they had been cut off from their old governments, which remained under Spanish rule. The prefix Staats- marked them as dependent territories under the States, and the Generality claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over them, as over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonies, by right of conquest. They were exploited with heavy taxes and levies. Westerwolde and Wedde were a generality land from 1594 to 1619 before joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drenthe">Drenthe</a>, though a member of the Union, also lacked a seat in the States-General.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Generality Lands ceased to exist after the French occupation and the proclamation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> in 1795, when States' Brabant became a département of the Batavian Republic and the other lands went into French départements. Under the United Kingdom of the Netherlands the former States' Brabant formed most of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a>, States' Flanders was joined to Zeeland, and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-guelders">Upper Guelders</a> and Overmaas became part of the province of Limburg, the rest going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Generality Lands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Leicester left in 1587 the States General stopped seeking a monarch and governed as a republic of seven provinces, Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, Gelderland, Overijssel, Friesland and Groningen, with conquered lands to the south ruled in common as the <b>Generality Lands</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When the surrounding lands came under the Dutch Republic, Ravenstein remained a Catholic enclave, where priests and believers from the <b>Generality lands</b> could worship freely.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-ravenstein">Lordship of Ravenstein</a></li></ul></section>
